s, no daily record lows","records":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","change":"Lincoln's days got warmer. Its nights didn't.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 46\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>46\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 53\u00b0F, and the record is 85\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>22\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Lincoln's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Lincoln has set <strong>54 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 70 that chance alone would give, and <strong>101 record highs</strong>, more than the 70 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (76). Lincoln's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on July 25, 1936.","t_chg":"Lincoln's days got warmer. Its nights didn't.","p_chg":"Across 139 complete years: Lincoln's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.12 to +0.02) that includes zero. Lincoln's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.29), about +2.9\u00b0F since 1887.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 3 months fell. Highs rose in 5 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Lincoln's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.20 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 29.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1951 (42.17 in); the driest, 1936 (14.09 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}